# Gang of Four Design Patterns

Use GoF patterns where they reduce coupling and clarify intent — never pattern-for-pattern's sake.

## Principles

- Favor **composition over inheritance**
- **Program to interfaces**, not implementations
- Name roles explicitly: `FormSubmissionStrategy`, `NavigationFacade`, `ScrollObserver`

## When to Use

| Category | Use when |
|----------|----------|
| **Creational** (Factory, Builder, Singleton) | Object creation is complex or varies by context |
| **Structural** (Facade, Adapter, Decorator) | Simplifying subsystems or extending behavior without subclassing |
| **Behavioral** (Strategy, Observer, Command, Template Method) | Algorithms, events, or workflows need to vary or decouple |

## This Project (HTML/CSS/JS)

- **Facade** — one entry module wires nav, animations, forms (`SiteApp.init()`)
- **Observer** — scroll/intersection events notify UI components
- **Strategy** — swappable handlers (e.g. mock form vs Formspree vs API)
- **Template Method** — shared page lifecycle with per-page hooks

## Examples

```javascript
// ✅ Strategy — swappable form backends
class FormspreeStrategy {
  submit(data) { /* ... */ }
}
class MockFormStrategy {
  submit(data) { /* show success UI */ }
}

// ✅ Facade — single public API
const SiteApp = {
  init() {
    Navigation.init();
    ScrollAnimations.init();
    ContactForm.init(new FormspreeStrategy(endpoint));
  }
};

// ❌ Avoid — monolithic script with tangled DOM + fetch + scroll logic
```

## Don't

- Force patterns into static markup-only changes
- Add abstraction layers with only one implementation
- Use Singleton for global mutable state without clear need
